  {
    name: "giuseppe_garibaldi",
    displayName: "Giuseppe Garibaldi",
    type: "Anti-aircraft Cruiser",
    armaments: [
      {
        label: "Main Battery",
        value: "4x2 135mm/45 Ansaldo Model 1938",
      },
      {
        label: "Anti-aircraft Guns",
        value: "4x4 76mm/62 OTO-Melara Sovrapposto",
      },
      {
        label: "Anti-aircraft Guns",
        value: "4x2 40mm/70 OTO-Breda",
      },
    ],
    description: "This is a 1950s proposal to rebuild the cruiser Giuseppe Garibaldi as an anti-aircraft cruiser, predating the Terrier missile conversion that was eventually carried out. The plan would have re-armed the ship with new automatic guns and the projected quadruple 76 mm Sovrapposto mounting, but that quadruple mount was never produced, and the twin version that did enter service aboard the Centauro class proved unreliable and was soon superseded. The conversion was never carried out; the artist reconstructed the proposal on the basis of the modernised Garibaldi.",
    author: "Tzoli",
    links: [
      {
        name: "Giuseppe Garibaldi AA Cruiser Design",
        url: "https://www.deviantart.com/tzoli/art/Giuseppe-Garibaldi-AA-Cruiser-Design-1221169920",
      },
      {
        name: "Wikipedia — Italian cruiser Giuseppe Garibaldi (1936)",
        url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_cruiser_Giuseppe_Garibaldi_(1936)",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "bolzano",
    displayName: "Bolzano",
    type: "Heavy Cruiser",
    armaments: [
      {
        label: "Main Battery",
        value: "4x2 203mm/53 Ansaldo Model 1929",
      },
      {
        label: "Secondary Battery",
        value: "6x2 100mm/47 OTO Model 1928",
      },
      {
        label: "Anti-aircraft Guns",
        value: "4x1 40mm/39 Vickers-Terni Model 1917",
      },
      {
        label: "Torpedoes",
        value: "2x4 533mm",
      },
      {
        label: "Seaplanes",
        value: "3x IMAM Ro.43",
      },
    ],
    description: "The third ship of the Trento class, but with a Zara-style forecastle, a Pola-style bridge and the catapult moved amidships between the funnels, so her design differs enough that she is often treated on her own. She was torpedoed by British submarines in 1941 and again in August 1942; the second hit ran her aground near Panarea, and when she was towed to La Spezia her repairs were still incomplete. Germany captured her after the Italian armistice in 1943, and she was sunk in harbour in June 1944 by Italian frogmen using human torpedoes; the wreck was raised and broken up after the war.",
    author: "Gemini 3.6 Flash",
    links: [
      {
        name: "RN Bolzano [Heavy Cruiser] (1933)",
        url: "https://www.the-blueprints.com/blueprints/ships/ships-italy/92154/view/rn_bolzano_heavy_cruiser_1933/",
      },
      {
        name: "Wikipedia — Italian cruiser Bolzano",
        url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_cruiser_Bolzano",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "muzio_attendolo",
    displayName: "Muzio Attendolo",
    type: "Light Cruiser",
    armaments: [
      {
        label: "Main Battery",
        value: "4x2 152mm/53 Ansaldo Model 1929",
      },
      {
        label: "Secondary Battery",
        value: "3x2 100mm/47 OTO Model 1928",
      },
      {
        label: "Anti-aircraft Guns",
        value: "4x2 37mm/54 Breda Model 1932",
      },
      {
        label: "Anti-aircraft Guns",
        value: "4x2 20mm/65 Breda Model 1932",
      },
      {
        label: "Torpedoes",
        value: "2x2 533mm",
      },
      {
        label: "Seaplanes",
        value: "2x IMAM Ro.43",
      },
    ],
    description: "The second ship of the Raimondo Montecuccoli class, the third batch of Italy’s Condottieri light cruisers, named after the 15th-century condottiero Muzio Attendolo Sforza. This batch traded a little speed for a real side belt and turret armour. While intercepting the Pedestal convoy in August 1942 she was torpedoed by HMS Unbroken and lost her entire bow, but damage control brought her back to port; before repairs were finished, a US B-24 bomb hit her at Naples on 4 December 1942 and she capsized. The wreck was raised and broken up after the war.",
    author: "Gemini 3.6 Flash",
    links: [
      {
        name: "RN Muzio Attendolo [Light Cruiser] (1941)",
        url: "https://www.the-blueprints.com/blueprints/ships/ships-italy/89549/view/rn_muzio_attendolo_light_cruiser_1941/",
      },
      {
        name: "Wikipedia — Italian cruiser Muzio Attendolo",
        url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_cruiser_Muzio_Attendolo",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "bartolomeo_colleoni",
    displayName: "Bartolomeo Colleoni",
    type: "Light Cruiser",
    armaments: [
      {
        label: "Main Battery",
        value: "4x2 152mm/53 Ansaldo Model 1926",
      },
      {
        label: "Secondary Battery",
        value: "3x2 100mm/47 OTO Model 1928",
      },
      {
        label: "Anti-aircraft Guns",
        value: "4x2 37mm/54 Breda Model 1932",
      },
      {
        label: "Torpedoes",
        value: "2x2 533mm",
      },
    ],
    description: "The second ship of the Giussano class and the first batch of the Condottieri light cruisers. Built for speed, she carried only a very thin side belt and ran about 40 knots on trials. In the Battle of Cape Spada on 19 July 1940 she and her sister Giovanni dalle Bande Nere met the Australian light cruiser Sydney and a British destroyer flotilla; a shell in her engine room left her without power or steering, and British destroyers finished her with torpedoes. About 121 of her crew were killed and some 555 were rescued. The drawing shows her as she was in 1940, without catapult or seaplane.",
    author: "Sam#28",
    links: [
      {
        name: "RN Bartolomeo Colleoni [Cruiser] (1933)",
        url: "https://www.the-blueprints.com/blueprints/ships/ships-italy/15225/view/rn_bartolomeo_colleoni_cruiser/",
      },
      {
        name: "Wikipedia — Italian cruiser Bartolomeo Colleoni",
        url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_cruiser_Bartolomeo_Colleoni",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "FF33E",
    displayName: "Friedrichshafen",
    type: "Reconnaissance Seaplane",
    armaments: [],
    description: "The Friedrichshafen FF.33e was the principal production reconnaissance variant of a German twin-float seaplane developed for the Imperial Navy during World War I. Powered by a 150 hp Benz Bz.III, it traded armament for a radio transmitter and had a maximum speed of about 124 km/h. Roughly 180 FF.33e aircraft were built.",
    author: "Unknown",
    links: [
      {
        name: "Wikipedia - Friedrichshafen FF.33",
        url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrichshafen_FF.33",
      },
    ],
  },
    {
    "name": "Ar196",
    "displayName": "Ar 196",
    "type": "Reconnaissance Floatplane",
    "armaments": [
      {
        "label": "Machine Gun (Fixed Forward, Cowling)",
        "value": "1 x 7.92mm MG 17"
      },
      {
        "label": "Machine Gun (Rear Cockpit)",
        "value": "1 x 7.92mm MG 15"
      }
    ],
    "description": "The Arado Ar 196 was the standard Kriegsmarine shipboard observation floatplane and the only German seaplane in service throughout the Second World War. The twin-float low-wing monoplane was catapult-launched from capital ships and cruisers and also flew coastal reconnaissance and anti-submarine patrols from shore bases, with 541 built. Maximum speed was about 332 km/h and range about 1,080 km, with a rugged airframe and good water handling. In game it is a scout armed as an A-2/A-5 with one fixed forward 7.92mm MG 17 in the cowling and one flexible 7.92mm MG 15 in the rear cockpit; the A-5's two 20mm MG FF/M wing cannon and bomb load are not configured and are not listed in the reference.",
    "author": "Unknown",
    "links": [
      {
        "name": "Wikipedia - Arado Ar 196",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arado_Ar_196"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    name: "Potez631",
    displayName: "Potez 631",
    type: "Heavy Fighter",
    armaments: [
      {
        label: "Cannons (Fixed Forward, Under Fuselage)",
        value: "2 x 20mm HS.404",
      },
      {
        label: "Machine Gun (Rear Cockpit)",
        value: "1 x 7.5mm MAC 1934",
      },
    ],
    description: "The Potez 631 C.3 was a twin-engine, three-seat heavy fighter introduced by the French Air Force in 1938. Powered by two Gnome-Rhône 14M engines, it had a maximum speed of about 440 km/h and carried two forward-firing 20mm HS.404 cannon in under-fuselage gondolas plus a 7.5mm MAC 1934 machine gun in the rear cockpit. It was slower and climbed worse than contemporary single-engine fighters, but served in day escort, night-fighter and fighter-direction roles during the 1940 Battle of France.",
    author: "Unknown",
    links: [
      {
        name: "Wikipedia - Potez 630 family",
        url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potez_630",
      },
      {
        name: "History of War - Potez 631",
        url: "https://www.historyofwar.org/articles/weapons_potez_631.html",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "LN401",
    displayName: "LN 401",
